<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Continental Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> was the assembly of delegates from the thirteen American colonies that met from May 1775, after fighting had begun at Lexington and Concord. It created the Continental Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, adopted the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>, and governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until 1781, when it became the Congress of the Confederation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress.jpg" alt="Second Continental Congress" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Edge Pine / Edward Savage · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congress_voting_independence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Second Continental Congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 10 May 1775, with representatives of 12 of the 13 colonies, after the first battles of the Revolutionary War at Lexington and Concord on 19 April. It succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> of September and October 1774, which had sent entreaties to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Intolerable Acts</a> and created the Continental Association to boycott British goods. Many delegates had attended the first Congress. They elected Peyton Randolph president and Charles Thomson secretary, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> and John Hancock were notable arrivals. Randolph was soon recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, Hancock succeeded him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> joined the Virginia delegation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> endorsed the Congress in July 1775.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At first the Congress acted as the de facto government. On 14 June 1775 it voted to create the Continental Army from the militia units around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, and the next day it unanimously named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> its commanding general. On 6 July it approved the Declaration of Causes for taking up arms, and two days later delegates signed the Olive Branch Petition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>. The king, who had already proclaimed on 23 August a state of open and avowed rebellion, refused to receive it.</p><p>The Congress had no legal authority from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> but appointed ambassadors, signed treaties, raised armies, borrowed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> called Continentals. It could not levy taxes and had to ask the states for funds, supplies and troops, which they often ignored.</p><p>In May 1776 it recommended that colonies without governments inclined to independence form new ones. On 7 June Richard Henry Lee offered the resolution declaring the colonies independent, and the Congress created three committees to draft a declaration, a model treaty and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>. It approved the resolution of independence on 2 July 1776 and the Declaration of Independence on 4 July. In the winter of 1776 to 1777 it moved to Baltimore, and from late September 1777 to York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, when British troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Congress passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a> on 15 November 1777, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> ratified first, on 16 December 1777. Each state was given one vote, and western land claims remained with the states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, the last holdout, ratified on 2 February 1781, after Virginia gave up its claims north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>. On 1 March 1781 the body became what is often called the Confederation Congress. During its years as provisional government it managed the war effort, secured diplomatic recognition and support from foreign nations and resolved state land claims west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Continental Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 July 1776 the <b>Second Continental Congress</b> adopted the Declaration of Independence, drafted mainly by Thomas Jefferson, which justified separation by appeal to natural rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Continental Congress</b> created the United States Post Office by decree, after printers and the new political leadership had overthrown the London-oriented imperial postal service.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>General George Washington, newly appointed commander-in-chief of the Continental Army and dispatched by the <b>Second Continental Congress</b>, took command of the Patriot soldiers camped on Cambridge Common, now regarded as the birthplace of the American army.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Continental Congress</b> took control of the Patriot forces outside Boston, forming the Continental Army, and John Adams nominated George Washington as its commander-in-chief.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-14">14 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
